<description>
Keep the larger sprite from each separated panel and overlay those sprites into one 5x5 canvas.

Input structure:
- The input is a 5x17 grid: three 5x5 panels separated by one-cell black columns.
- Each panel has the same background color and exactly one foreground color split into two 4-connected components.

Rule:
- In each panel, discard the smaller foreground component and keep the unique largest component as the sprite.
- Process panels left to right, placing each sprite at its panel coordinates on a 5x5 background canvas.
- If a sprite would cover an already placed sprite, slide it by the unique nearest in-bounds offset that avoids overlap.

Key insight:
- The extra same-colored component in each panel is a distractor; the final answer is the non-overlapping overlay of the three largest components.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "move_in_order",
  "src": "largest 4-connected foreground object in each 5x5 panel",
  "dst": "overlay onto one 5x5 canvas in left-to-right panel order",
  "transform": "discard the smaller same-color object from each panel; when a later object overlaps earlier objects, slide it by the nearest in-bounds non-overlapping offset",
  "grid_shape": "5x17 input with separator columns -> 5x5 output"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 5x17 input (three 5x5 panels separated by single black/0 columns) -> 5x5 output
  background: 8 (azure), separator: 0 (black)
  panels:
    count: 3, each 5x5, separated by 1-cell-wide black columns at cols 5 and 11
    each panel has exactly 1 foreground color (not 0 or 8), split into exactly 2 four-connected components
    the 3 panel foreground colors are all distinct
  sprites:
    largest component per panel is the sprite (kept); smaller component is the distractor (discarded)
    largest must be strictly larger than smaller (no ties)
    sprite size: 2-9 cells; distractor size: 1-4 cells; distractor < sprite always
    both sprite and distractor are individually 4-connected
  output_rule:
    process panels left-to-right; place each sprite at its original panel coordinates on a 5x5 bg canvas
    if sprite overlaps already-placed cells, slide by unique nearest Manhattan-distance shift that avoids overlap and stays in bounds
    5/6 examples have no overlap (all 3 sprites fit at original coords); 1/6 has exactly 1 shift [observed]
    total foreground in output: 9-16 cells

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Choose 3 distinct foreground colors (not 0 or 8).
    2. Build the 5x5 output canvas by placing 3 non-overlapping 4-connected sprites (sizes 2-9) on bg=8. Place them one at a time; for each, pick a random 4-connected polyomino shape, then choose a valid position with no overlap. Record each sprite's final cell coordinates.
    3. Optionally (low probability) shift the 3rd sprite by a small offset to create overlap input; otherwise use sprites at their output positions directly as panel positions.
    4. For each panel, place the sprite cells at the chosen coordinates. Then add a smaller 4-connected distractor (size 1 to sprite_size-1) of the same color, placed on empty bg cells, ensuring the panel has exactly 2 four-connected components of that color.
    5. Assemble the 5x17 grid: panel0 | black col | panel1 | black col | panel2.
